ANALYSIS — why the gate is BLOCKED, and why it is NOT this surface
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. The committed project.yml routes `TeslaSync/feature-views` (and dashboard-widgets,
   and now shared-surfaces) into the iOS + macOS APP targets with no `*.Tests.swift`
   exclusion. ~170 `feature-views/*.Tests.swift` files therefore compile INTO the app
   target and each `import XCTest`. An app target has no XCTest in its module search
   path, so the compile fails: "Unable to resolve module dependency: 'XCTest'".
2. This is PRE-EXISTING and repo-wide: the first (and only) error is the unmodified
   committed file feature-views/AIRestorePanel.Tests.swift. .github/workflows/apps-apple.yml
   itself documents the project as a "no-op-but-valid until the P4 Apple program adds a
   buildable ... .xcodeproj" — i.e. the full app is known to be not-yet-buildable.
   Fixing it requires routing every `*.Tests.swift` from the app targets to the test
   targets (TeslaSyncTests / TeslaSync-macOSTests) — a P4-core/infra change spanning all
   170+ pre-existing test files, explicitly OUTSIDE the scope of this single shared-surface
   prompt (and risky to attempt blind, as it would surface unbounded pre-existing breakage).
3. This surface follows the EXACT established convention (Tests alongside the surface, same
   as AIRestorePanel/ChatMessageItem/AiUsageCard), so the eventual repo-wide test-routing
   fix resolves it together with the other 170 files — with no change to this surface.
4. The surface is nonetheless fully verified, independent of the blocked full-app gate:
     - swiftc -typecheck against the real design system: 0 errors (STEP 3)
     - 39/39 unit tests PASS in a real XCTest target (STEP 4)
     - swiftlint surface: 0 violations (STEP 5)
     - swiftformat surface: clean (STEP 6)
     - placeholder: 0 (STEP 7)
     - 0 compile errors attributable to the surface in BOTH full-app builds (STEP 8)

